<item>
            <title>[LU-20467] Jenkins job that builds Lustre against the latest mainline kernel using Clang</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20467</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;We should have a Jenkins job that builds Lustre with Clang against the latest mainline kernel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, we should grab the latest kernel from &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.kernel.org/&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://www.kernel.org/&lt;/a&gt; (in this case, 7.1). The version should be determined from a file in the build tree (perhaps a top-level META file or in one of the contrib/lbuild/ scripts).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Second, we should install the latest and greatest Clang compiler onto the build host (&lt;a href=&quot;https://clang.llvm.org/).&quot; class=&quot;external-link&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow noopener&quot;&gt;https://clang.llvm.org/).&lt;/a&gt; Clang 22 is the latest stable release, but 23 is preferred - if possible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We should use `make LLVM=1 modules_prepare` on the kernel git tree allow Lustre to be built against this kernel without have to compile a completely new kernel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, we should use `./autogen.sh &amp;amp;&amp;amp; ./configure LLVM=1 --with-linux=/path/to/linux --with-linux-objs=/path/to/linux/objs &amp;amp;&amp;amp; make` to build Lustre against this new Clang-built kernel.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
